  "cause": "**On Monday, two men who both claim the last election went to the President's residence to ask for the same job.** **Benjamin Netanyahu** and **Gadi Eisenkot** were each to make their competing case to **President Isaac Herzog** for the first chance to build a governing coalition — the formal step that follows the Central Elections Committee's final results. The arithmetic is fixed and it does not produce a majority: **Netanyahu's Likud and its allies hold 53** of the Knesset's 120 seats, **Eisenkot's opposition bloc 48**, and the two **Arab-led parties hold a record 19** — in a vote with the **highest turnout in nearly three decades**. Neither bloc can govern without those 19 seats, and neither has said it will sit with them. Herzog, who has urged parties to engage with an open heart to avoid a fresh election, must now hand one of them a mandate of up to 42 days. **The campaign has not ended; it has moved into the certificate.** Likud has alleged **foreign interference** and said the Arab-led parties **\"stole\"** the election after its majority fell — a claim for which no evidence has been published. The opposition accuses Netanyahu of trying to **\"delegitimise\"** the election. BBC analysis lists four scenarios for breaking the deadlock, and the most likely of them, by its own account, is **another election**. Meanwhile the file the next government inherits is not waiting: Israeli strikes in **Gaza** killed at least **eight** people on Sunday and Monday, including a **four-year-old boy** — three killed by a missile beside **Cairo School** in Gaza City's al-Rimal neighbourhood, and **five Hamas-run police officers**, aged 23 to 53, killed by a drone strike in **al-Mawasi** near Khan Younis. The Israeli military said it struck \"military operatives\" and has published no evidence. A ceasefire has been in force since **October last year**; **1,580** people have been killed since it began, according to Gaza's health ministry, whose figures the UN considers reliable; **74,594** have been killed since the war started. The Trump-brokered peace plan has **stalled**.",
  "bullets": [
   "**A mandate is not a majority, and the 42-day clock is already the story** — 53 seats, 48 seats, 19 seats, and a president obliged to choose whom to try first; the direct effect is that the first candidate will almost certainly fail and the second will inherit the failure; the compound consequence is that the mandate becomes a device for *distributing blame* rather than for forming a government, and the country enters a third election cycle in two years having proved only that the arithmetic is stable",
   "**The bloc that won a record share of the vote is being described as a theft** — \"stole the election\", \"foreign interference\", published without evidence; the direct effect is that 19 legitimate seats are loaded with illegitimacy before any negotiation begins; the compound consequence is that the only available coalition arithmetic requires the consent of the party being accused of fraud, so every future government rests on a partner whose mandate its own partner denies — a structure that cannot survive one budget",
   "**Delegitimising the count is the cheapest way to win the next count** — the accusation is unfalsifiable and therefore unanswerable, which is what makes it useful; the direct effect is that the opposition must now defend an election result rather than use it; the compound consequence is that the same move is available at every level of Israeli politics, and a state whose elections can be declared stolen by the loser has transferred the decision from the voter to the courtroom and the studio",
   "**The Gaza file is being administered by a caretaker with every incentive to do nothing** — 1,580 dead since a ceasefire that is not a ceasefire, eight more this weekend, a stalled plan, and a prime minister whose authority is provisional; the direct effect is that the ceasefire's terms are enforced by nobody and violations are priced at nothing; the compound consequence is that every week of deadlock adds to a toll the *next* government will inherit as a baseline, and a baseline is the hardest thing in politics to lower",
   "**\"Military operatives\" without evidence is a doctrine, not an incident report** — two strikes, nine dead including a four-year-old, five police officers in a designated humanitarian zone, and an assertion of legitimacy with nothing attached; the direct effect is that each strike is unfalsifiable and therefore unaccountable; the compound consequence is that the standard of proof collapses for both sides at once, and a post-war accounting — the thing that would let anyone live beside this — becomes impossible to conduct",
   "**The hinge is whether the 19 seats are treated as voters or as a problem, and whether the mandate is used to build or to blame** — the moment a candidate says plainly that a coalition resting on Arab-led parties is legitimate, which is the only sentence that makes 61 seats possible; the moment Likud's allegation is either evidenced or withdrawn; the moment Herzog's 42 days are used to publish the price of each bloc's support rather than to run down the clock toward a third election; and the moment the Gaza ceasefire is given a monitor and a violation ledger instead of a stall. §10:83 is unusually blunt about which of these is the creational question: people who do not share your opinion \"do not need your persecution, but rather your protection.\""
  ],
  "verse": "Do not persecute people of your kind (human beings) against all right and in enmity if they do not share your willingness (sense), are not of your people, of your skin colour or of your opinion and are not of your avowal (knowledge or belief), because however and whoever they may be, they are the same as you and therefore people of your kind (human beings), and as such they do not need your persecution, but rather your protection.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 10, Verse 83",
  "hinge": "The cause is not the arithmetic. It is the choice — by Netanyahu, whether a bloc that lost its majority accepts a result it cannot reverse or converts resentment into a permanent claim of theft; by Eisenkot, whether the 19 seats are asked for openly or worked around by legislation; by Herzog, whether the 42-day mandate is used to force a published price from each side; and by the parties who would sit with either, whether they will govern or merely veto. §10:83 does not say that agreement is required; it says the people who disagree with you are the same as you and need your protection, not your persecution. Nineteen seats, 120 in the house, one certificate to sign — and the only question that matters is whether the largest turnout in thirty years produced a government or an accusation."
