  "cause": "**On Saturday in Paris a police officer's leg was on fire, and by Monday the question was what to call it.** French prosecutors opened an **attempted murder investigation** after petrol bombs were thrown at gendarmes during the third consecutive weekend of protests. The officer — a member of the **National Gendarmerie**, whose unit had been recalled from leave — told **Le Figaro** he suffered burns to the ankle and minor hand injuries, that his unit \"suddenly found ourselves facing several hundred hostile, well equipped adversaries\", and that the device was \"particularly sophisticated\", with \"a far greater burning capacity than a standard Molotov cocktail\". **Fourteen other officers** were wounded, some struck by paving stones. Interior Minister **Laurent Nuñez** said throwing a Molotov at a police line is \"what I call a murder attempt\". **No one has been arrested** for the attack caught on camera. The demonstrations themselves were the largest yet in a movement that began in late September: **more than 320,000 people** across France, and **more than 770 arrests** — roughly **half** the previous week's total. Organisers, the **Gilets Jaunes**, say dozens of protesters have been seriously injured, many by \"grenades\"; France suspended one type of stun grenade after a **15-year-old's hand was blown off** in early October, but **Le Monde** reports police have continued using a different model. **The same night, the German version went from protest to looting.** In **Frankfurt** — the country's financial hub — shops were looted, windows smashed and Molotov cocktails thrown at emergency services, with water cannon deployed against people targeting emergency workers with fireworks, stones and bottles; the windows of a **gun shop** were smashed and **two weapons seized** after an arrest; a large section of the main shopping street was closed. In **Cologne** officers were attacked near the main station, with bottles and a bicycle thrown; in **Hamburg** people dressed in black set off fireworks and burned barricades, and **120** were arrested; in **Berlin** a smaller evening protest left **27 officers injured** and up to **56** detained. Frankfurt, Munich and Berlin police counted as many as **18,000** demonstrators — roughly half the organisers' estimate. The movement, **Gen Z regelt** (\"Gen Z takes charge\"), marched against underfunded schools, the cost of living, cuts to education, social services, culture and healthcare, and conscription; spokesperson **Lina Friedrich** said the aim is \"peace and freedom, rather than war and coercion\", better health and education, no compulsory military service, and \"a fairer distribution of the burden\". Hesse's interior minister **Roman Poseck** attributed the Frankfurt violence to \"around **100** clearly identified left-wing extremists\" and warned that \"France must not be held up as a model\". Federal Interior Minister **Alexander Dobrindt** told **Bild** that police had prevented \"violence like that seen in Paris\" and called for **\"harsh punishments\"**. Protests also took place in Italy.",
  "bullets": [
   "**The vocabulary is being chosen before the facts, and it will decide the response** — \"what I call a murder attempt\" is a minister's characterisation, now attached to a charge that carries decades; the direct effect is that one image sets the legal frame for an entire movement; the compound consequence is that once the frame is attempted murder, the policing answer escalates by necessity, and fourteen officers wounded by paving stones become the justification for a posture that will produce more wounded on both sides",
   "**\"Around 100 extremists\" is a way of not counting 17,900 people** — an 18,000-strong demonstration reduced to a hundred named infiltrators; the direct effect is that the file closes without a single demand being answered; the compound consequence is that the movement learns the only way to be counted is to be feared, which is the opposite of what a petition does — and the state has just demonstrated that petitions obtain nothing",
   "**\"Harsh punishments\" is a policy that answers a fiscal cause with a penal one** — school funding, living costs, service cuts and conscription, answered by prosecution; the direct effect is that the next demonstration is policed as a security event rather than received as a grievance; the compound consequence is that the *demand* is now unanswerable without looking like a concession to violence, so the government has made its own most reasonable option politically expensive",
   "**A looted gun shop is the single most useful object either side could have been given** — two weapons seized, a suspect arrested, and an image that writes the next curfew, the next ban and the next deployment; the direct effect is that the movement's own rejection of violence becomes unverifiable; the compound consequence is that a demand-set with majority support is now associated with firearms, and association is what elections are decided on",
   "**Europe is converging on the same answer in different capitals** — 320,000 in France, 18,000 counted in Germany, clashes in Italy, Portugal clearing a tent camp over a rental bill; the direct effect is that nine countries are now managing a youth cost-of-living movement with police; the compound consequence is that the convergence is not coordination — the movements have no centre and the states have no shared remedy, so the same grievance escalates in each country on its own clock, and each government measures its success by the arrests it made",
   "**The hinge is whether the demand-set is answered in a budget or in a courtroom, and whether the violence is counted by someone neither side appoints** — the moment a school-funding figure with a date is published; the moment a minister meets the organisers rather than the \"around 100\"; the moment the grenade injuries are investigated by a body whose findings both the interior ministry and the Gilets Jaunes will accept; the moment the attempted-murder file is pursued against whoever threw the device and not used to characterise a march. §7:85 puts the obligation where the grievance actually is: provide for those who give evidence of their need, \"so that things may be fair to them and you\"."
  ],
  "verse": "Provide for (feed and clothe) those who give you signs (evidence) of their poverty (need) so that things may be fair to them and you, and so that no powerful rain of disgust (inhumanity) may sweep down over you which will drown you in shame and guilt, such that you find a miserable end in the estimation of people of your kind (fellow human beings).",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 7, Verse 85",
  "hinge": "The cause is not the petrol bomb and it is not the looted shop. It is the choice — by the French government, whether an attempted-murder file is a prosecution or a doctrine; by the German interior ministry, whether \"harsh punishments\" is the answer to a school budget; by the organisers, whether a movement that rejects violence can police its own margins or will keep handing the state its best photograph; and by both publics, whether a 15-year-old's hand and a gendarme's ankle are counted in the same ledger. §7:85 does not ask the state to agree with the protesters; it says that need, once shown, creates an obligation, and that refusing it brings down a \"rain of disgust\" on the refuser. Three weekends, 320,000 people, one burning trouser leg — and a government deciding whether to answer an invoice or an accusation."
