  "cause": "**On Friday, 109 soldiers left Mogadishu, and the longest continuous African Union deployment in Somalia began to end.** The first contingent of **Uganda People's Defence Forces** troops departed after army chief **Gen Muhoozi Kainerugaba** announced the pull-out: *\"As we promised, we have started withdrawing UPDF from Somalia. After 20 years of a lot of blood and sacrifice, we are handing over security to our Somali brothers\"* (X). The withdrawal is **phased and due to be complete by August 2027**. Uganda was the **first African country to deploy** to Somalia, in **2007**, under **AMISOM**, and its troops helped drive **al-Shabab** out of Mogadishu and other major towns, then served through **ATMIS** and its successor **AUSSOM**, established in 2025 to strengthen Somali institutions. About **4,500** Ugandan troops remain under the AU mission, alongside roughly **2,000** deployed under **separate bilateral arrangements** that are *not* being withdrawn — so the handover is partial, and the part that stays is the part that answers to Kampala rather than to the AU. **The mission they are leaving is itself being wound down.** **AUSSOM** faces uncertainty over its future funding amid plans to wind down **US and UN support**; a **12-month transition plan** is due to begin in **January**, giving Somalia and the AU a year to find alternative financiers. The **UN's political mission** in Somalia is closing at the same time. Al-Shabab's insurgency has run for more than two decades and continues; Somali forces are to take \"full responsibility\" for security under a schedule written by the bodies now withdrawing. The same week, the UN's **MINURSO** peacekeeping mission in **Western Sahara** ended after **35 years** — a second long deployment closing in the same news cycle, with Morocco celebrating the defeat of the resolution that would have extended it.",
  "bullets": [
   "**A 20-year security guarantee is being removed on a timetable written by the guarantor** — 109 troops first, August 2027 for the rest, and a bilateral 2,000 staying behind outside AU command; the direct effect is that the transition is real and irreversible in one direction only; the compound consequence is that the forces which remain answer to a national capital, so the AU mission's authority ends before its soldiers leave — the mission becomes fiction while the presence stays",
   "**The funding cliff arrives months before the transition plan can possibly work** — AUSSOM's backing from the US and UN winding down, a 12-month plan starting in January, and no identified successor financier; the direct effect is that the pay, fuel, medical evacuation and air support of an active counter-insurgency are at risk in the same quarter; the compound consequence is that armies unpaid and unsupported do not dissolve quietly — they fragment into the militias and checkpoints that al-Shabab recruits from, and the withdrawal becomes a force multiplier for the enemy it was deployed against",
   "**Two missions closing in one week is a signal, not a coincidence** — AUSSOM winding down and MINURSO ending after 35 years; the direct effect is that the UN's long-presence model is contracting on two continents at once; the compound consequence is that the conflicts those missions contained are now owned entirely by the parties to them, which is either sovereignty or abandonment depending on whether anyone fills the gap — and no one has said who will",
   "**\"Handing over security to our Somali brothers\" is the correct sentence and the hardest one** — 20 years of external forces holding the front line produced a state that has not yet held it alone; the direct effect is that the test is now unavoidable and will be conducted in public; the compound consequence is that a failed handover is attributed to Somali incapacity rather than to the schedule, so the country inherits both the risk and the blame for a timeline it did not set",
   "**The armies leaving are also the armies that were paid** — Uganda's deployment has been a source of revenue and prestige for the UPDF; the direct effect is that withdrawal reduces both; the compound consequence is that the incentive to keep some presence — the bilateral 2,000 — is financial as well as strategic, and a \"partial\" withdrawal with a paid remainder is the shape of arrangement that can persist for another decade while being described as an end",
   "**The hinge is whether the transition plan is funded before it is implemented, and whether the remaining forces sit under one command** — the moment the January plan arrives with a pledged budget line rather than a hope; the moment the bilateral contingent is placed under AU or Somali command or declared as what it is; the moment Somalia's own forces are paid, fed and paid *on time*, since that is the only counter-insurgency capability that compounds; and the moment the departing states say publicly what they are leaving behind and to whom. §28:38 describes the mechanism precisely, and it cuts in both directions: the responsibility is always the first thing to be borne, and it is routinely pushed onto \"other fellow human beings\" because bearing it is heavy."
  ],
  "verse": "Bearing the responsibility is always the first thing you have to do, but the by far largest number amongst you human beings of Earth, you push off the bearing and exercising of the responsibility onto your imaginary godheads, tin gods, angels, saints and to idolised human beings as well as onto your next ones or other fellow human beings, because on the one hand you see the responsibility as a burden with which you do not want to encumber yourselves and, on the other hand, because the bearing of the responsibility is, out of cowardice, too much for you, or because you think that you are not responsible yourselves.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 28, Verse 38",
  "hinge": "The cause is not the withdrawal and it is not al-Shabab. It is the choice — by Uganda, whether the bilateral contingent is an orderly residual force or an undeclared continuation; by the AU and the UN, whether AUSSOM's successor is funded before January rather than after; by the states winding down support, whether a counter-insurgency that has cost 20 years is left with a budget line or a press release; and by Somalia's government, whether the forces it now owns are paid on time. §28:38 does not condemn those who hand over responsibility — it identifies the moment of pushing it off as the moment the burden compounds. Twenty years, 109 soldiers down a road out of Mogadishu, and a date in August 2027 that nobody has yet chosen to fund."
